  The general rule: every path in the logger either produces a record or produces nothing, and never propagates.

  [1mTesting.[0m This is where the no-build-step constraint bites, so let me be straight about it. The valuable logic — level
  resolution, deny-list redaction, circular handling, ring-buffer wraparound — is pure and very worth testing. But
  [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m is a browser global script, so Node can't [38;5;153mrequire[39m it.

  The way out that doesn't cost anything: [38;5;153mnode:test[39m (built into Node, no install) loads [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m with [38;5;153mreadFileSync[39m and
  evaluates it in a [38;5;153mnode:vm[39m context holding fake [38;5;153mwindow[39m, [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, and [38;5;153mconsole[39m objects. Assertions run against the
  fake console and the returned buffer. Zero dependencies, no build step, and it tests the real file rather than a copy.
  The DOM wiring in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m stays unit-untested — that part I'd verify by opening the page.

  Since this repo has no tooling configured at all, that's a choice for you rather than an assumption for me:

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which tooling should I set up alongside the logger? (Cheapest to add now, before there's code to retrofit.) → [39m
     [38;5;246mUnit tests via node:test + vm (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Design approved in all three sections. Writing the spec.

